1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,058
Lesson 5.8.

2
00:00:02,438 --> 00:00:05,198
Energy accounting in
heterogeneous systems.

3
00:00:06,317 --> 00:00:09,891
In lesson 5.3 we computed
that the Z1 spends at

4
00:00:09,891 --> 00:00:13,009
most seventy-four femtojoules per update.

5
00:00:13,389 --> 00:00:17,457
But a chip never works alone: someone
has to send it the parameters,

6
00:00:17,457 --> 00:00:20,927
read its samples and do the
digital computation around it.

7
00:00:21,307 --> 00:00:25,160
The energy of the full system can be
very different from that of the chip.

8
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:27,028
Today we learn to count it.

9
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:30,097
We split the energy into three parts.

10
00:00:30,477 --> 00:00:34,148
The fabric: the pbit
updates inside the chip.

11
00:00:34,528 --> 00:00:37,321
Input and output: the
bits that cross the chip

12
00:00:37,321 --> 00:00:40,722
boundary, such as biases going
in and states coming out.

13
00:00:41,102 --> 00:00:45,641
And the digital glue: the computation
on the CPU, GPU or FPGA that

14
00:00:45,641 --> 00:00:50,112
prepares the biases, controls
sampling and processes the samples.

15
00:00:50,492 --> 00:00:52,800
The total energy is the sum of the three.

16
00:00:53,917 --> 00:00:57,989
We need reference costs, and we
must say where they come from.

17
00:00:58,369 --> 00:01:03,888
According to Horowitz's estimates
for a forty-five nanometer process, a

18
00:01:03,888 --> 00:01:08,553
thirty-two-bit floating-point
multiply costs 3.7 picojoules,

19
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:14,636
an add 0.9, and an eight-bit
integer multiply only 0.2.

20
00:01:15,016 --> 00:01:19,179
Reading thirty-two bits from DRAM
costs about six hundred forty

21
00:01:19,179 --> 00:01:23,211
picojoules: moving data costs
much more than operating on it.

22
00:01:23,591 --> 00:01:28,830
For off-chip communication we will assume
ten picojoules per bit, an estimate.

23
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:32,794
Let's count one sample on the
Z1 with a thousand sweeps.

24
00:01:33,174 --> 00:01:37,213
The fabric: two hundred sixty-nine
thousand pbits times a thousand

25
00:01:37,213 --> 00:01:41,130
sweeps times seventy-four
femtojoules, about twenty microjoules.

26
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:46,634
Input and output: eight bias bits going
in and one state bit coming out per

27
00:01:46,634 --> 00:01:50,939
pbit, at ten picojoules per bit,
about twenty-four microjoules.

28
00:01:51,319 --> 00:01:55,613
The glue: if a digital network
computes each bias with a hundred

29
00:01:55,613 --> 00:02:00,578
floating-point multiply-adds, that is
one hundred twenty-four microjoules.

30
00:02:00,958 --> 00:02:05,704
The total is one hundred sixty-eight,
and the fabric is only twelve percent.

31
00:02:06,817 --> 00:02:08,667
How do we reduce what is not fabric?

32
00:02:09,047 --> 00:02:14,613
First, with low-precision glue: with
eight-bit integer arithmetic, the glue drops

33
00:02:14,613 --> 00:02:19,423
to six microjoules, and the fabric
becomes forty percent of the total.

34
00:02:19,803 --> 00:02:24,646
Second, by amortizing input and output:
with more sweeps per readout, the

35
00:02:24,646 --> 00:02:28,826
fabric equals input and output
at around twelve hundred sweeps.

36
00:02:29,206 --> 00:02:32,549
Third, by reading summaries
instead of full states.

37
00:02:32,929 --> 00:02:36,006
And finally, the long-term
goal: removing the

38
00:02:36,006 --> 00:02:39,358
digital glue with more
functions inside the chip.

39
00:02:40,483 --> 00:02:43,095
Every energy number must carry a label.

40
00:02:43,475 --> 00:02:47,114
Measured, if it came from an
instrument on a real device.

41
00:02:47,494 --> 00:02:49,984
Simulated, if it came
from a physical model.

42
00:02:50,364 --> 00:02:53,629
Projected, if it extrapolates a
design that does not exist yet.

43
00:02:54,009 --> 00:02:57,778
And estimated, if it is an
order-of-magnitude calculation like today's.

44
00:02:58,158 --> 00:03:02,925
In this course, the seventy-four
femtojoules are a bound derived from

45
00:03:02,925 --> 00:03:07,140
specifications, Horowitz's costs
are estimates, and the final

46
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:11,354
comparison is an estimate: never
present it as a measurement.

47
00:03:12,483 --> 00:03:17,434
In practice, before any claim one writes
the sum of the three terms, reports

48
00:03:17,434 --> 00:03:22,059
the energy per sample with the number
of sweeps and the glue precision,

49
00:03:22,439 --> 00:03:27,013
and compares with a digital baseline on
the same task and at the same quality.

50
00:03:27,393 --> 00:03:30,521
In the final project, every
figure will carry its label.

51
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:36,131
Common mistakes: quoting the fabric
energy as if it were the system's.

52
00:03:36,511 --> 00:03:39,128
Ignoring the digital network
that computes the biases.

53
00:03:39,508 --> 00:03:42,690
Comparing energies at
different qualities or tasks.

54
00:03:43,070 --> 00:03:47,049
And presenting a projection or an
estimate as if it were a measurement.

55
00:03:48,150 --> 00:03:51,705
In summary: the energy of
a heterogeneous system is

56
00:03:51,705 --> 00:03:54,982
fabric plus input and
output plus digital glue.

57
00:03:55,362 --> 00:03:58,260
In our example the fabric was only twelve

58
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:01,441
percent, rising to forty
with eight-bit glue.

59
00:04:01,821 --> 00:04:06,391
Every number must say whether it is
measured, simulated, projected or estimated.

60
00:04:07,517 --> 00:04:11,615
To practice: compute the fabric
energy with ten thousand sweeps.

61
00:04:11,995 --> 00:04:15,509
Find how many sweeps make fabric
equal to input and output.

62
00:04:15,889 --> 00:04:19,759
Compare the glue with eight-bit
integers and with floating point.

63
00:04:20,139 --> 00:04:23,655
And correctly label the
seventy-four femtojoules.
